-- title:
--   Locally bounded level-N modular functions are integral over ℂ[j]
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and let $x$ be a Laurent series over $\mathbb{C}$. Two hypotheses are imposed. First, $x$ lies in [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange ℂ (ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldFull N)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), the intermediate field of $\mathbb{C}((q))/\mathbb{C}$ generated over $\mathbb{C}$ by the image, under the coefficientwise ring map [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb ℂ`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) induced by $\mathbb{Q}\to\mathbb{C}$, of the subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the set [`ModularCurve.divisorExpansions N`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L297) of the series $\mathrm{qExpand}_{\mathbb{Q}}\,d\,(\mathtt{jq})$ for the nonzero divisors $d$ of $N$, where $\mathtt{jq} = q^{-1}\cdot \mathtt{jNumQ}$ is the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant over $\mathbb{Q}$. Second, for every $\tau$ in the upper half plane $\mathfrak{H}$ the function $z \mapsto \lVert \mathtt{ModularCurve.realize } N\, x\, z\rVert$ is bounded above along the punctured neighbourhood filter $\mathcal{N}[\neq]\,\tau$; here $\mathtt{realize } N\, x\, \sigma$ is defined to be $g(\sigma)/h(\sigma)$ for a chosen weight $k$ and pair $(g,h)$ of modular forms of weight $k$ on $\Gamma_0(N)$ with $h(\sigma)\neq 0$ and $x\cdot\widetilde{h} = \widetilde{g}$ (the tilde denoting $q$-expansions taken with width $1$), and $0$ when no such data exist. The conclusion is that $x$ is integral over the $\mathbb{C}$-subalgebra of $\mathbb{C}((q))$ generated by the single element $\mathtt{coeffEmb ℂ jq}$, that is, over $\mathbb{C}[\tilde\jmath\,]$.
--
--   This is the classical statement that a modular function of level $N$ with no poles on $\mathfrak{H}$ is integral over $\mathbb{C}[j]$, reflecting the fact that the affine curve $Y_0(N)$ is the normalisation of the affine $j$-line in the field of level-$N$ modular functions. It is used in the passage from formal $q$-expansions to points of the modular curve, via [`ModularCurve.ComplexPlaceDictionary.exists_pt_eq_of_mem`](thm.html#ModularCurve.ComplexPlaceDictionary.exists_pt_eq_of_mem); the proof presents $x$ as a ratio of $q$-expansions of modular forms of equal weight on $\Gamma_0(N)$ and invokes integrality of $\widetilde{g}/\widetilde{\Delta}^m$ over $\mathbb{C}[E_4^3/\Delta]$.
-- source:
